\name{InferenceContinQuantileRegr}
\alias{InferenceContinQuantileRegr}
\title{Quantile Regression Inference for Continuous Responses}
\description{
Fits a linear quantile regression, \eqn{Q_\tau(Y_i \mid x_i) = x_i^\top\beta_\tau},
for a continuous response, estimating \eqn{\beta_\tau} by minimizing the
asymmetric ("pinball" / "check") loss
\deqn{\hat\beta_\tau = \operatorname*{arg\,min}_\beta \sum_{i=1}^n \rho_\tau(y_i -
  x_i^\top\beta), \qquad \rho_\tau(u) = u\left(\tau - \mathbb{1}[u < 0]\right),}
via \pkg{quantreg}'s simplex method (\code{quantreg::rq}/\code{rq.fit(...,
method = "br")}). The treatment coefficient is the estimated shift in the
\eqn{\tau}-th conditional quantile of the response attributable to treatment,
holding any other covariates in \code{model_formula} fixed; by default
\code{tau = 0.5}, so this is median regression (robust to outliers and
distributional skew relative to mean-based estimators, at the cost of losing
the mean-shift interpretation away from \eqn{\tau = 0.5}).

Standard errors use \pkg{quantreg}'s Powell-style \code{"nid"} (non-i.i.d.,
kernel-based sparsity/local-density estimator) sandwich covariance when
available, with fallback to the simpler \code{"iid"} estimator if needed.
Inference (confidence intervals, p-values) is based on the resulting
asymptotic normal approximation, not an exact finite-sample distribution.

This class requires the \pkg{quantreg} package, which is listed under
\code{Suggests} and is not installed automatically with \pkg{EDI}.
Install \pkg{quantreg} manually before use.
}
\section{Small-sample behaviour}{

How fast the size (and coverage) error of each procedure is expected to vanish as the
effective sample size \eqn{n_{eff}}{n_eff} grows, from EDI's convergence-rate registry
(\code{inference_convergence_expectation()}). \emph{Exact} procedures are valid at every
n by construction; the others are asymptotic, so a small-sample excess is expected, and
its constant is not given by an order. The table is the expectation for a balanced
complete-randomization design; it is not a measured size. Reference keys are explained
under References.

\tabular{lllllll}{\strong{Procedure} \tab \strong{Regime} \tab \strong{Error order} \tab \strong{Effective n} \tab \strong{Regularity} \tab \strong{Basis} \tab \strong{References} \cr
\code{rand} \tab finite sample \tab exact \tab subjects \tab regular \tab theory \tab [Fisher1935], [LehmannRomano2005] \cr
\code{wald} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-2/3})}{O(n_eff^-2/3)} \tab subjects \tab nonparametric rate \tab literature \tab [HallSheather1988], [Koenker1994], [Koenker2005] \cr
\code{jackknife} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} \tab subjects \tab regular \tab assumed default \tab [ShaoTu1995] \cr
\code{bootstrap} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} \tab subjects \tab regular \tab assumed default \tab [Hall1992] \cr
\code{bayes_boot} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} \tab subjects \tab regular \tab assumed default \tab [Rubin1981], [Lo1987] \cr
\code{rand_bootstrap} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} \tab subjects \tab regular \tab assumed default \tab [Hall1992], [Kallus2018]}

The point estimate's bias is of order \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} (assumed default: [CoxSnell1968]).

Rows with basis \emph{assumed default}: the order shown is the procedure's rate in a
regular parametric model, and the cited works establish it for that case only. EDI has not
derived it for this class.

Regularity \emph{nonparametric rate}: the standard error needs a nonparametric (kernel sparsity) estimate, so the rate is slower than the parametric one.

Design-specific differences from the table:
\itemize{
\item On any design with unequal allocation (\code{prob_T} not 0.5), for \code{wald}, \code{jackknife}, \code{bootstrap}, \code{bayes_boot}, \code{rand_bootstrap}: the effective n is the size of the smaller arm.
\item On \code{DesignFixedBlockedCluster}, \code{DesignFixedCluster}, for \code{wald}, \code{jackknife}, \code{bootstrap}, \code{bayes_boot}, \code{rand_bootstrap}: the effective n is the number of clusters.
}
